Your recursive call is missing a base case to stop the recursion. Add a condition to return 1 when n reaches 0 or 1.

```cpp
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) { // Base case: factorial of 0 and 1 is 1
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * fact(n - 1); // Recursive call
    }
}
```

This will ensure the function stops recursing when n equals 0 or 1, preventing a stack overflow.

Your code will lead to a stack overflow because each recursive call creates a new stack frame, and if `n` is large, this will exceed the maximum recursion depth.

To fix this, you need to ensure that your recursive calls eventually terminate. One way to do this is by adding a base case to stop the recursion when `n` reaches 0 or 1.

Here's how you can modify your function:

```cpp
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) return 1; // Base case: factorial of 0 and 1 is 1
    else return n * fact(n - 1);
}
```

This way, when `n` reaches 0 or 1, the function will stop calling itself recursively and start returning values back up the call stack.

```c
#include <iostream>

// Return n! using recursion.
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
        return 1;
    else
        return n * fact(n - 1);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << fact(5) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Return n! using recursion.
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 1)
        return 1;
    // what type of value should be returned here when n is 0?
}

